  constellations:{
    ursaMajor:{ name:'Ursa Major', text:'Ursa Major is the most recognisable constellation. Its seven bright stars form the Big Dipper, a guide for travellers through the ages. The two outer stars of the bowl point straight to Polaris. In reality these stars are tens of light-years apart.' },
    ursaMinor:{ name:'Ursa Minor', text:'At the tip of Ursa Minor’s tail shines Polaris, the North Star — it always points north, guiding sailors for millennia. Polaris lies 433 light-years away and shines 2,000 times brighter than the Sun.' },
    orion:{ name:'Orion', text:'Orion the Hunter. The three belt stars line up perfectly, and nearby glows a great nebula where new stars are born. Red Betelgeuse on the hunter’s shoulder is 700 times the size of the Sun — it could explode as a supernova at any moment.' },
    cassiopeia:{ name:'Cassiopeia', text:'Cassiopeia draws a giant “W” across the sky. In ancient legend, she was a proud queen. In 1572 a famous supernova blazed here — so bright it was visible even in daylight.' },
    cygnus:{ name:'Cygnus', text:'Cygnus the Swan flies along the Milky Way. Its brightest star, Deneb, is one of the most powerful stars known, thousands of light-years away. This constellation is home to the first confirmed black hole — Cygnus X-1.' },
    scorpius:{ name:'Scorpius', text:'Scorpius, Orion’s foe in ancient myth — which is why the two are never seen in the sky together. At its heart burns the red supergiant Antares, the “rival of Mars”, named for its ruddy colour.' }
  },
