    cosmosTitle:'Fascinating facts about the cosmos',
    cosmosSets:{
      planets:{ title:'Facts about the Solar System', facts:[
        'The Solar System formed 4.6 billion years ago from a collapsing cloud of gas and dust.',
        'All planets orbit in nearly the same plane and direction — evidence of their shared origin.',
        'Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is now in interstellar space — the farthest human-made object.',
        'Sunlight takes 8 minutes to reach Earth, and over 4 hours to reach Neptune.',
        'The Solar System circles the Milky Way’s centre once every 230 million years, at 828,000 km/h.'
      ]},
      stars:{ title:'Facts about the stars', facts:[
        'The Milky Way holds 100–400 billion stars — counting one per second would take thousands of years.',
        'A star’s colour reveals its temperature: blue stars are the hottest, red ones relatively “cool”.',
        'Starlight travels for centuries or millennia to reach us — looking at the sky is looking into the past.',
        'A teaspoon of neutron-star matter weighs billions of tonnes.',
        'Stars in a constellation are not related — they merely appear in the same direction from Earth.'
      ]},
      visual:{ title:'Facts about cosmic scenery', facts:[
        'Auroras are born when solar-wind particles crash into Earth’s atmosphere.',
        'The Orion Nebula is visible to the naked eye on a clear night.',
        'At the Milky Way’s centre sits Sagittarius A* — a black hole of 4 million solar masses.',
        'Staring at one dark spot, the Hubble telescope found more than 10,000 galaxies there.',
        'The universe is expanding — distant galaxies recede from us at nearly the speed of light.'
      ]},
      blackholes:{ title:'Facts about black holes', facts:[
        'Time slows near a black hole: an hour for a traveller beside one can equal years for a distant observer.',
        'Nothing escapes the event horizon — not even light. That is why they are “black”.',
        'The first photo of a black hole came in 2019: the giant in galaxy M87, weighing 6.5 billion Suns.',
        'Black holes slowly “evaporate” via Hawking radiation — even small ones outlive the universe’s age trillions of times over.',
        'If the Sun became a black hole, Earth’s orbit would not change — a black hole is not a “vacuum cleaner”, just very dense mass.'
      ]},
      eclipse:{ title:'Facts about solar eclipses', facts:[
        'A total eclipse recurs at any given spot on Earth only about once every 375 years.',
        'During an eclipse the temperature drops 5–10 degrees, and animals prepare for sleep.',
        'There are 2 to 5 solar eclipses a year, but totality is visible only along a narrow track.',
        'The Moon drifts 3.8 cm from Earth each year — in millions of years total eclipses will end.',
        'Helium was discovered in 1868 during an eclipse, from the spectrum of the corona.'
      ]},
      objects:{ title:'Facts about small bodies', facts:[
        'If the entire asteroid belt were gathered together, it would weigh less than the Moon.',
        'Up to 100 tonnes of cosmic dust and micrometeorites fall to Earth every day.',
        'Comets are “dirty snowballs”: near the Sun their ice vaporises, growing tails millions of km long.',
        'Beyond the Kuiper Belt lies the Oort Cloud, holding trillions of comet nuclei.',
        'Some asteroids have moons of their own: tiny Dactyl orbits the asteroid Ida.'
      ]}
    },
